 Comments sort by `created_at` ascending, then ID ascending.
 
+### Cursor pagination
+
+For large histories, opt in with nested `page[size]` instead of the scalar
+`page`/`per_page` parameters. This works on **all existing collections**:
+`/tickets`, `/requests`, both `/tickets/:id/comments` and
+`/requests/:id/comments`, `/users`, `/staff`, `/invitations`, `/groups`, and
+`/group_memberships`.
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+```
+
+Records retain their existing JSON representation. Cursor responses replace the
+page-number metadata with `meta` and `links` (no total count is calculated):
+
+```json
+{"tickets":[],"meta":{"has_more":false,"after_cursor":null,"before_cursor":null},"links":{"next":null,"prev":null}}
+```
+
+* Every populated page includes `before_cursor` for its **first** record and
+  `after_cursor` for its **last** record, even at the beginning/end of the list.
+* Follow `links.next` or send `page[after]=AFTER_CURSOR` to browse forward.
+  Follow `links.prev` or send `page[before]=BEFORE_CURSOR` to browse backward.
+  Backward pages are still returned in the collection's normal display order.
+* `meta.has_more` reports whether there are more records **in the requested
+  direction**: forward on the first request or with `after`, backward with
+  `before`. It is not a synonym for “next link exists” on a backward request.
+* Links are relative URLs that preserve filters and page size. Continue sending
+  the bearer session and `X-Helpdesk-Account` headers. Unavailable links are
+  `null`; an empty page has null cursors and links and `has_more: false`.
+* `page[size]` defaults to **25** when omitted in cursor mode and must be an
+  integer from **1 through 100**. Clients may change size between requests.
+  Supply at most one cursor. Mixing `per_page` with nested `page`, unknown nested
+  page keys, invalid sizes, empty/malformed/tampered cursors, or using a cursor
+  for a different tenant, viewer, collection, parent, or effective filter scope
+  returns `400` with the usual `invalid_request` JSON error. Treat cursors as
+  opaque strings; changing filters requires a fresh first-page request.
+
+**Stable ordering:** cursor-mode tickets and requests sort by immutable
+`created_at DESC, id DESC`, rather than the mutable activity timestamps used by
+page-number mode. Cursor-mode comments sort by `created_at ASC, id ASC`; all
+other collections sort by `id ASC`. Timestamp ties are broken by ID, with full
+database timestamp precision in the cursor. New replies, assignment changes,
+and private notes do not move existing records across a cursor boundary.
+Deleting the boundary record does not invalidate its cursor.
+
+Cursor pagination is a **live view, not a snapshot**. Newly created records can
+appear on their side of a boundary; deleted records and records that stop
+matching a filter disappear. Current authorization and visibility are reapplied
+on every request. Refresh from the beginning to see newer tickets. Seek queries
+use indexed, strict tuple comparisons and fetch at most `size + 1` records, plus
+at most one bounded existence query for the opposite link. They do not use
+`OFFSET` or `COUNT`, so deeper pages do not discard growing prefixes. Arbitrary
+filters, especially text searches, can still affect query cost.
+
